{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 110,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s been 120 years since Henry Cavendish\nmeasured the gravitational constant with a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5490,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair of lead balls suspended by a wire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8740,
    "dDurationMs": 4009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fundamental nature of gravity still eludes\nour best minds - but those secrets may be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12749,
    "dDurationMs": 3501,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealed by turning back to the Cavendish\nexperiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16250,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That steampunk contraption may even reveal\nthe existence of extra dimensions of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27080,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity is by far the most familiar of the\nfour fundamental forces - after all, we wrestle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32460,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with it every day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33860,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s no surprise that it was the first force to get a decent mathematical description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39600,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with Isaac Newton’s Law of Universal Gravitation\nback in the 17th century."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44070,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what is surprising that nearly 500 years\nlater, gravity remains the most mysterious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49540,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the four forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51220,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It just doesn’t seem to gel with the other\nthree forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One big difference is that the strength of\ngravity is vastly weaker than the other forces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59280,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- I’m talking 10^24 times weaker than even the weak nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66320,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This mismatch is known as the hierarchy problem,\nand understanding the puniness of gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72670,
    "dDurationMs": 5949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may help us figure out what gravity really\nIS, and to unify it with the rest of the forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78619,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But like I said, despite the mysteries about\nwhy gravity works the way it does, our current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84390,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theories still describe how gravity behaves\nremarkably well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88619,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein's general theory of relativity has\na perfect track record predicting the behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93670,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity in terms of the warping of the\nfabric of space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97900,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even Newton’s old theory works great\nin almost all situations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102530,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And deviations from Newton’s description\nmay point the way to an understanding deeper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107689,
    "dDurationMs": 1611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than Einstein’s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109300,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key is in understanding the inverse square\nlaw."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113060,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton’s law of gravitation is an example\nof such a law - basically, the strength of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118060,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational force drops off with the\nsquare of the distance between two massive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122790,
    "dDurationMs": 1010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123800,
    "dDurationMs": 3359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we have a massive object, we can depict\nthe gravitational force (field) from this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127159,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object as little arrows pointing towards the\nobject."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130729,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The density of the arrows at a given distance\ndetermines the strength of the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134700,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135700,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So as you get further away, those arrows are\nmore and more spread out - their density,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140040,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the strength of gravity drops."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142860,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The arrows get spread out over the surface\nof a sphere with the gravitating body in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147730,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149040,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the density and the strength of the gravitational\nfield drop proportional the surface area of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155140,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that sphere - that’s 4 pi r^2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158580,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s where the r^2 comes from on the bottom\nof Newton’s law of gravitation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163920,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So gravity sort of gets diluted in strength\nbecause it’s increasingly spread out over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169250,
    "dDurationMs": 3819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a 2-D spherical surface within our 3-D space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173069,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If our universe were 2-dimensional - say,\na flat plain - then gravity would be spread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178190,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out over the circumference of concentric circles\n- circumference is 2 pi r - so gravity would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184050,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just be inversely proportional to radius - it\nwould drop off more slowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189160,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if our universe had more dimensions, say\n4, then gravity would spread out over the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193769,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“surface” of a 4-D hypersphere - that\nsurface would be 3-D, so gravity would follow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200210,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an inverse cube law - that’s a faster drop\noff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204460,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, gravity drops off as 1/r to some\npower - and that power is the number of spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210550,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimensions minus one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212740,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cool - so we have a way to test how many dimensions\nthere are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216190,
    "dDurationMs": 6970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton’s inverse square law for gravity\ntells us there are 2+1 = 3 spatial dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223160,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I … guess we already knew that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226380,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except for the fact that some of the most\npromising approaches to unifying gravity with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230519,
    "dDurationMs": 5312,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other forces actually propose that there\nare MORE than 3 dimensions of space, and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235831,
    "dDurationMs": 5438,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these additional dimensions may explain the\nhierarchy problem - the relative weakness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241269,
    "dDurationMs": 1761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the gravitational force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243030,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s talk about dimensions a bit more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245460,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We perceive a 3-D universe, but most of our\nmovements from day to day are only in 2 of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250409,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them - walking across your 2-D floor, driving\naround the 2-D surface of your town or city."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255709,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We explore a 2-D landscape embedded in a 3-D\nworld."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260139,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we perceive the 3rd dimension, but perhaps\nour 3 spatial dimensions are similarly embedded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266389,
    "dDurationMs": 3511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a higher dimensional space that we can’t\nperceive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269900,
    "dDurationMs": 2909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well actually, that’s probably not the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272809,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we saw in a previous episode how a\nparticular gravitational wave detection from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277669,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LIGO seemed to rule out the possibility of\nextra spatial dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282180,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a caveat - that experiment only\nruled out extra large dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288539,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by large I mean they stretch out over\nastronomical scales like the familiar 3 spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293909,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294909,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s another possibility - the extra\ndimensions could be compactified - in other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300589,
    "dDurationMs": 2751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words, wound up very small."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303340,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case, gravity would only deviate from\nthe inverse square law on the tiny scales"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309139,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these compactified dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312009,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Take another look at the surface you’re\non right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314589,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems like a 2-dimensional surface, but\nwe all know that the apparently 2-D ground"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318629,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is made up of 3-D particles, and if we zoomed\nin on a seemingly flat floor to small enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324159,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale, we would see a wildly varying, 3-dimensional\nlandscape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328749,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t notice this because of the vast\ndifference in scales between us and that landscape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334040,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another example would be a long, thin tube."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337069,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It looks 1-dimensional from a distance, but\nup close you see that it’s really a 2-D"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341189,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface - extended in one dimension, but looped\nin the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345599,
    "dDurationMs": 5211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the sense in which there may\nbe more than 3 spatial dimensions in this universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350810,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 extended dimension, but others that are\nlooped - or “compactified” as we say in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356409,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357719,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most famous example of compactified extra\ndimensions is in string theory, and in modern"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362479,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superstring theory there are 6 additional\nspatial dimensions, coiled-up tight at every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367469,
    "dDurationMs": 2621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point in the extended 3-D universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370090,
    "dDurationMs": 5309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the idea of compact extra dimensions is\nmuch older than string theory - first proposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375399,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by Oscar Klein in the 1920s as part of what\nbecame Kaluza-Klein theory - one of the first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381349,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attempts to unify electromagnetism with gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385499,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although we’ve never detected these extra\ndimensions, they seem a promising potential"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389550,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solution to unifying gravity with the other\nforces - and in the process explaining gravity’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395710,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weakness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396710,
    "dDurationMs": 3539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are different ways of thinking about\nhow compactified extra dimensions can effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400249,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strength of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402029,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One is that you can imagine the gravity trying\nto propagate through multiple dimensions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406219,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would dilute it more quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408319,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can also think about it as gravity producing\nexcitations within those extra dimensions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413289,
    "dDurationMs": 1731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that sap its energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415020,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On large scales, gravity would still follow\nthe inverse square law, but would be diluted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421300,
    "dDurationMs": 3899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the hidden extra dimensions - and that\ncould explain its relative weakness compared"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425199,
    "dDurationMs": 1601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compared to other forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426800,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And on small scales these extra dimensions\nwould actually change the way gravity falls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431149,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off with distance - but to see that you’d\nneed to test the strength of gravity on length"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436529,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scales that are within spitting distance of\nthose compactified dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441569,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, to search for these hidden dimensions,\nwe need a way to test gravity on very small"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447529,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "length scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449229,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lots of different approaches have been tried,\nbut today I want to tell you about one of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453469,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most promising - because it’s also the\noldest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456349,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s actually the OG - the experiment first\nused to measure the strength of the force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461030,
    "dDurationMs": 4449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity - or the G in Newton’s law of\ngravitation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465479,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the Cavendish experiment, performed\nby Brit Henry Cavendish in the late 18th century."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472139,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The apparatus for the experiment was actually\ndesigned and built by another British scientist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477449,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Mitchell - the same guy who first hypothesized\nthe existence of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482689,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Mitchell died before he could conduct\nthe experiment, and so Cavendish inherited"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487159,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and improved the device, and actually made\nthe measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490729,
    "dDurationMs": 4851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Cavendish experiment looked like this:\na 6-ft rod suspended from its center by a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495580,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long, thin, metal wire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496860,
    "dDurationMs": 3199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lead ball was attached to each end of the\nrod."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500059,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The whole contraption was then free to rotate\non the wire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503279,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an example of a torsion pendulum - where\nthe restoring force of the twisted wire can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509580,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cause the whole apparatus to oscillate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512610,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amazing thing about the torsion pendulum\nis that it’s incredibly sensitive - if the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517430,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wire is very thin, then very tiny forces will\ninduce a large twist before being halted by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523389,
    "dDurationMs": 1981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the restoring force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525370,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of twist tells you precisely how\nmuch force was applied to rotate it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530580,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, Cavendish placed large masses near each\nof the lead balls at the ends of the rods.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535529,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gravitational attraction between these\nmasses and the balls would then rotate the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539019,
    "dDurationMs": 5651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "torsion pendulum ever so slightly, and by\nreading off the amount of rotation Cavendish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544670,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made the first measurement of the gravitational\nconstant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548480,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The device was so sensitive that it had to\nbe protected from the slightest breeze, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553379,
    "dDurationMs": 5561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even the gravitational influence of other\nbodies - including Cavendish’s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558940,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He measured the displacement through a telescope\nmounted into a hole in the device’s shed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564650,
    "dDurationMs": 4359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This allowed him to measure a force of gravity\nbetween the masses which was something like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569009,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 million times smaller than the force the\nlead balls felt due to the earth below."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574060,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The meticulous care paid off—Cavendish measured\na gravitational constant that agrees with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579070,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our modern value, with 1% uncertainty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582540,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His measurement remained the most precise\nfor over 100 years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587550,
    "dDurationMs": 6370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not only did this contribute to our understanding\nof gravity, but also arguably marked the beginning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593920,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a grand tradition in physics of making\nhigh-precision measurements to learn about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598769,
    "dDurationMs": 2851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fundamental nature of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601620,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Modern Cavendish experiments look quite a\nbit different than Mitchell and Cavendish’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607120,
    "dDurationMs": 1159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first apparatus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608279,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The experiment is now typically done in a\nvacuum, with precise temperature control and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613190,
    "dDurationMs": 2699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrostatic and magnetic shielding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615889,
    "dDurationMs": 5361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The geometry of modern devices allow more\nprecise positioning between the test masses,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621250,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the displacement of the masses is measured\nwith far more sophistication than with a telescope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626089,
    "dDurationMs": 2291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a hole in the wall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628380,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some cases, rotating masses produce oscillations\nin the torsion pendulum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These incredible advances have led to a modern\nmeasurement with 6 digits of precision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640410,
    "dDurationMs": 6219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These advances have also allowed us to place\nthe pendulum masses and the test masses extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646629,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close together, as close as 50 micrometers\n- less than the width of a human hair."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653029,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that’s still quite a bit larger than\nany likely compactified extra dimensions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657870,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we’d still expect a tiny and perhaps\ndetectable deviation from the inverse square"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662290,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "law several orders of magnitude larger than\nthe size-scale of those dimensions - depending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667709,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on other factors - most importantly depending\nthe strength with which gravity interacts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672959,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with these extra dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674629,
    "dDurationMs": 2881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, how many dimensions are there?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677510,
    "dDurationMs": 1569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, still just three."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679080,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The inverse square law seems to hold down\nto the current limits of our measurements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683220,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while that may seem anticlimactic, it’s\nactually extremely useful, ruling out some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688580,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theories and constraining the space of possibility\nfrom others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are still nowhere near being able to probe\nthe size-scale of the extra dimensions from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697690,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theory though, and so string theorists\ncan keep on theorizing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703480,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what's next?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705620,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, to build better Cavendish-style experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710520,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things get complicated below about 1 micrometer\nin separation, that’s where the Casimir"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716490,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force comes into play."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718370,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the negative pressure due to the exclusion\nof quantum vacuum modes, or virtual particles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724410,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between two very closely separated plates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726730,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are other ingenious approaches that\nwe can come back to another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, we'll have to content ourselves with\nour merely 4-dimensional universe - four..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738480,
    "dDurationMs": 7419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what were they again? space, space space,\n... time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745899,
    "dDurationMs": 3101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this is pretty amazingly unbelievable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749000,
    "dDurationMs": 6189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This little show about hardcore physics and\nastrophsyics just hit 2 million subscribers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755189,
    "dDurationMs": 4441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly - it warms all our hearts to know\nthere are so many kindred nerdy spirits out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759630,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760630,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should, like, all hang out some time - you\nknow, after all this is over."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764910,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OF course if we did hang out we’d be a whole city."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768120,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we looked it up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769410,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d be the 238th largest city in the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And growing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773620,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s right, we’re coming for you Maltan,\nPakistan - you’ve been chillin in rank 237"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779089,
    "dDurationMs": 1031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far too long."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780120,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, I just want to thank you from the\nbottom of my heart for your incredible support."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784959,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And speaking of support, I want to give an\nextra big thank you to our Patreon supporters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789930,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and most especially to Christina Oegren, who’s\nsupporting us at the quasar level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795110,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Christina, with your great generosity and\nexcellent taste in youtube shows, I hereby"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800160,
    "dDurationMs": 3419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appoint you mayor of spacetime city."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803579,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because no one said spacetime city was a democracy,\nand I think you’d do an amazing job."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809089,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In last week’s episode we talked about the\nstrange regions that lie beneath the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815079,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon of a rotating black hole - regions\nlike the Carter time machine and the infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821829,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string of parallel universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824199,
    "dDurationMs": 5461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unsurprisingly, thoughts were had and comments\nwere made."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829660,
    "dDurationMs": 7420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zoltan asks for clarification - really, confirmation\nof his hunch which is that the “multiverse” you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837080,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get from traveling through black holes has\nnothing to do with the multiverse predicted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841360,
    "dDurationMs": 1469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by quantum physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842829,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zoltan, you have it right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844499,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum multiverse is what you get when\nyou accept Hugh Everett's many worlds interpretation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849949,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which implies that the universe splits at\nevery quantum event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853649,
    "dDurationMs": 2891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That multiverse may or may not be real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856540,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the multiverse through the black hole arises\nfrom tracing the paths of spacetime through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862089,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole mathematically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864529,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'd say the second multiverse is less likely\nto be real than the quantum multiverse - it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870199,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost certainly a mathematical figment, arising\nfrom idealized types of black hole that don't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876899,
    "dDurationMs": 1761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878660,
    "dDurationMs": 7609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On a related note: Polygondwanaland asks how\nmuch of this weirdness is actually weirdness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886269,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how much is broken math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888509,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the universe on the other side of the\nblack hole is probably a figment in the math"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893430,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- which I suppose you can call broken math\nif you like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896690,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But really, the math is just fine - the problem\nis assigning physical reality after pushing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901600,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the math too far."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903050,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, the universe may be incredibly\nwell described and modeled by the math, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908439,
    "dDurationMs": 2531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that doesn't mean the universe is the math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910970,
    "dDurationMs": 5679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quality of the predictions of any model\ndepend on the assumptions that go into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916649,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in the case of the black holes, several\nunreasonable assumptions allow us to trace"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921779,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geodesics into alternate universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924889,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the parallel universe through\nthe non-rotating Schwarzschild black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929980,
    "dDurationMs": 3709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires the assumption that the black hole\nhas been there forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933689,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the string of universes through the rotating\nKerr black hole requires the assumption that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938879,
    "dDurationMs": 5221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the impossibly unstable inner structure of\nthe Kerr spacetime doesn't collapse immediately."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944100,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you're totally right, Polygondwanaland:\nin reality, impossible-seeming things like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949369,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an infinite string of universes or a time\nmachine indicate that the math may have led"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954869,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our physical understanding astray, and that's\neven more true when we see that the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960260,
    "dDurationMs": 4069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to conspire to prevent these things\nfrom existing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964329,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to wrap up - my favourite joke in the\ncomments that I wish I'd said in the episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968550,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969550,
    "dDurationMs": 2569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of \"You're in for a weird time.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972119,
    "dDurationMs": 5141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should have said \"I'm in for a weird space!\"\nbecause space and time switched places inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977260,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the blackhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978730,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least I can live vicariously through the\nsuperior wit of the brilliant Dr Poopstick."
    } ]
  } ]
}
